For I/O-bound services, use the Prometheus Adapter pipeline. Stages: Micrometer exposes → Prometheus scrapes → Prometheus Adapter translates to `custom.metrics.k8s.io` → HPA queries.

Micrometer provides `http.server.requests` automatically — no custom code. For specific signals, register a counter:

```java
@Component
public class OrderMetrics {
    private final Counter orderCounter;
    public OrderMetrics(MeterRegistry registry) {
        orderCounter = Counter.builder("orders_processed_total")
            .description("Total orders processed")
            .register(registry);
    }
    public void recordOrder() { orderCounter.increment(); }
}
```

Configure Prometheus Adapter to expose `orders_per_second` as a custom metric. The HPA then targets request rate per pod:

```yaml
metrics:
  - type: Pods
    pods:
      metric: {name: http_requests_per_second}
      target: {type: AverageValue, averageValue: "100"}
```

Scale up when each pod averages more than 100 requests/sec — a directly meaningful threshold correlated with business load. For queue-based workloads (Kafka consumers, SQS processors), use **KEDA** — it scales from zero based on queue depth, which HPA cannot do (HPA requires `minReplicas >= 1`).
